--- 3.2 What the restriction costs in precision ---
  brand - reputation at first exposure
    pooled model, position-specific : +0.7381  (SE 0.3124)
    n = 1 subsample only            : +0.9786  (SE 0.3445)
  Same quantity, two ways of getting it. The pooled model borrows the
  residual and person variance from all 1,473 answers; the subsample fit
  estimates them from 491. The point estimates are close, so the
  restriction was not biasing anything -- it was simply leaving the test
  unavailable, because nine separate regressions share no parameters.


=== 4. IS THE GAP REALLY DIFFERENT AT FIRST EXPOSURE? ===

The point estimates say the brand lead shrinks and reverses with position.
Whether that pattern is established is a separate question: it is the
three-way cue x manipulation x position interaction.

Data: long
Models:
m_no3: y ~ focal * x_f + focal * position + x_f * position + inv_app + inv_dl + inv_cat + (1 | subj_f)
m_sat: y ~ focal * x_f * position + inv_app + inv_dl + inv_cat + (1 | subj_f)
      npar    AIC    BIC  logLik -2*log(L)  Chisq Df Pr(>Chisq)
m_no3   19 5265.2 5365.8 -2613.6    5227.2                     
m_sat   23 5267.1 5388.9 -2610.6    5221.1 6.0719  4     0.1938

Also as the two-level version (first vs after others):
Data: long
Models:
m_c0: y ~ focal * x_f + focal * comp_f + x_f * comp_f + inv_app + inv_dl + inv_cat + (1 | subj_f)
m_c: y ~ focal * x_f * comp_f + inv_app + inv_dl + inv_cat + (1 | subj_f)
     npar    AIC    BIC  logLik -2*log(L)  Chisq Df Pr(>Chisq)
m_c0   15 5264.0 5343.4 -2617.0    5234.0                     
m_c    17 5263.6 5353.6 -2614.8    5229.6 4.3874  2     0.1115

So: the first-exposure ranking is established (§3); the claim that the
ranking CHANGES with position is not. Both statements are needed, and the
2023 work made the second one -- the M1-to-M2 'reversal' -- on the
strength of point estimates from disjoint samples.

--- 4.1 A correction to how this review reported the same term ---
The review quoted the three-way interaction as 'F = 0.64, p = 0.63'. That
is one of two numbers, not the test. Because the level of the cue is
assigned between respondents, aov() with Error(subject) splits the term
across the two strata, and prints it twice:


Error: subj_f
                    Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value Pr(>F)  
x_f                  1   20.0  20.037   4.434 0.0358 *
focal:x_f            2   24.4  12.225   2.705 0.0679 .
focal:position       4   56.9  14.233   3.149 0.0142 *
x_f:position         2   14.8   7.395   1.636 0.1958  
focal:x_f:position   4   42.1  10.515   2.327 0.0555 .
Residuals          477 2155.7   4.519                 
---
Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1

Error: Within
                    Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value   Pr(>F)    
focal                2  208.2  104.08  73.961  < 2e-16 ***
x_f                  1  139.3  139.29  98.985  < 2e-16 ***
position             2   11.3    5.67   4.028   0.0181 *  
focal:x_f            2   27.9   13.94   9.904 5.52e-05 ***
focal:position       4   12.9    3.21   2.284   0.0586 .  
x_f:position         2    6.9    3.45   2.450   0.0869 .  
focal:x_f:position   4    3.6    0.90   0.642   0.6325    
Residuals          965 1358.0    1.41                     
---
Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1

  between-subject stratum : F = 2.327, p = 0.0555
  within-subject stratum  : F = 0.642, p = 0.6325
  combined (mixed model)  : chi-square = 6.01, 4 df, p = 0.198

The conclusion does not change -- the interaction is not significant, and
the 2023 claim that it was remains withdrawn -- but the honest number is
the combined one. Quoting 0.63 alone reports the half of the term that
vanishes and omits the half that does not.


=== 5. VERDICT ===

What the 2023 design got right:
  * the first judgement is the only uncontaminated one, and the threat it
    protects against is visible in the data (§1);
  * on that quantity the ranking brand > reputation holds, and holds under
    every specification tried: +1.05 Likert points (p = 0.0035) and
    +1.28 log-odds (p = 0.002) in the thesis's own sample, and the same
    estimate from the pooled model (§3);
  * Model 3's comp_i interaction was the right instinct: it says the
    effect depends on whether comparison has happened.

What remains wrong, and is a smaller and more specific charge than the
review first made:
  * nine regressions on disjoint subsamples cannot test a difference
    between cues, and none was tested. The estimand did not require the
    fragmentation: one model with a position interaction delivers it and
    the test together (§3.2);
  * the ranking was then stated without its scope. 'Brand is the most
    effective' is supported for the first app seen; it is not supported as
    an average over the three judgements, where brand and reputation are
    indistinguishable (+0.20, p = 0.22);
  * M3's x_brand = 1.5028 was read as the effect 'independently from n'.
    With comp_i and its interaction in the model, that coefficient is the
    effect at comp_i = 0, i.e. first exposure again -- not an overall
    effect;
  * the M1-to-M2 'reversal' is not established: the interaction that would
    license it is not significant (§4).
